{"content":[{"c":["BAALI*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Hebrew title meaning “my lord” or “my master” (",{"href":"?bref=Hos.2.16","r":"Hos.2.16","rawR":"Hos.2.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hos.2.16","value":"Hos.2.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hos 2:16"},"). The title was rejected by God because of its association with the Canaanite Baal. God chose instead to be addressed with the Hebrew word ",{"c":["’ishi"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," “my husband,” which has a similar meaning but is untainted by pagan associations. Thus, in a prophetic play on words, God stressed his covenantal love for his people and emphatically rejected any implication that he was for Israel only what Baal was for the Canaanites. ",{"c":["See"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=BaalIdol_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"BaalIdol_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"BaalIdol_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Baal (Idol)"},"; ",{"href":"?item=GodNamesof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"GodNamesof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"GodNamesof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"God, Names of"},"."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Baali","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Hos 2:16","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Hos.2.16","rawQueryValue":"Hos.2.16","sourceRefExact":"Hos.2.16"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0f99de555221edbb98d3b44b9c50ba4f4f826ec503ceab21b786bd3f36ef7764","sourceItemName":"Baali","sourceItemOrder":14,"sourceMember":"Articles/B.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Baali","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Baali*","title":"Baali*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}